# Silent Error Fallback for Invalid Document IDs

Part of #60 (Security & Code Review Findings)

## Priority: 🔵 LOW

## Problem

Invalid UUID format errors are only logged at DEBUG level, making issues hard to diagnose.

**File:** `QueryService.java:117`

```java
try {
    documentRepository.findById(UUID.fromString(docId))
        .ifPresent(doc -> result.put(docId, doc.getIndexedAt()));
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    log.debug("Invalid document ID format: {}", docId);  // ❌ Only DEBUG level
}
```

## Issue

- DEBUG logs often disabled in production
- Invalid UUIDs indicate **data corruption** or **bug**
- Silent failure hides systematic problems

## When This Happens

1. Document indexing saves non-UUID document_id
2. Data migration corrupts IDs
3. Manual data manipulation errors
4. Version mismatch between indexer and query service

## Solution

```java
try {
    documentRepository.findById(UUID.fromString(docId))
        .ifPresent(doc -> result.put(docId, doc.getIndexedAt()));
} catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
    log.warn("Invalid document ID in chunk metadata: {} - {}", 
             docId, e.getMessage());  // ⚠️ WARN level

    // Consider metrics
    invalidDocumentIdCounter.increment();
}
```

## Better: Prevent Invalid IDs

```java
// In Document entity
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)  // Always valid
private UUID id;

// In chunk metadata
metadata.put("document_id", document.getId().toString());  // Type-safe
```

## Definition of Done

- [ ] Change log level from DEBUG to WARN
- [ ] Add metric counter for invalid IDs
- [ ] Investigate why non-UUID IDs exist
- [ ] Add validation at indexing time
- [ ] Consider data cleanup migration

